@* Home.razor — The landing page of StudentBasics.
   CONCEPT: Razor Pages & Static Rendering
   - @page "/" makes this the root route of the application.
   - Pages that don't need interactivity (no button clicks, no user input)
     can be rendered as plain HTML — no @rendermode directive needed.
*@

@page "/"

<PageTitle>Home – StudentBasics</PageTitle>

<h1>Welcome to StudentBasics 🎓</h1>
<p class="lead">
    A beginner-friendly Blazor app that walks you through the core concepts of
    building interactive web UIs with C# and Razor syntax.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>What you will learn</h2>

<div class="row row-cols-1 row-cols-md-2 g-4 mt-1">

    @* Each card below describes one Blazor concept demonstrated in this app. *@

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100 border-primary">
            <div class="card-header bg-primary text-white">📄 Razor Syntax</div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p class="card-text">
                    Razor lets you mix C# directly into your HTML using the <code>@@</code> symbol.
                    For example, <code>@@currentCount</code> renders the value of a C# variable
                    right inside the page.
                </p>
                <a href="/counter" class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm">See it on the Counter page →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100 border-success">
            <div class="card-header bg-success text-white">🖱️ Event Handling</div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p class="card-text">
                    Use <code>@@onclick</code>, <code>@@oninput</code>, and other Blazor event
                    directives to call C# methods when users interact with the page.
                </p>
                <a href="/counter" class="btn btn-outline-success btn-sm">See it on the Counter page →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100 border-warning">
            <div class="card-header bg-warning text-dark">🔗 Data Binding</div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p class="card-text">
                    <code>@@bind</code> creates a two-way link between a C# variable and an
                    input element — when the user types, the variable updates automatically.
                </p>
                <a href="/students" class="btn btn-outline-warning btn-sm">See it on the Student List →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100 border-info">
            <div class="card-header bg-info text-dark">✅ Forms & Validation</div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p class="card-text">
                    Blazor's <code>EditForm</code> component works with data annotations to
                    validate user input before it is submitted, showing helpful error messages.
                </p>
                <a href="/add-student" class="btn btn-outline-info btn-sm">See it on Add Student →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100 border-secondary">
            <div class="card-header bg-secondary text-white">🔀 Conditional Rendering</div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p class="card-text">
                    Use <code>@@if</code>, <code>@@else</code>, and <code>@@foreach</code> inside
                    Razor to show or hide content based on data — just like normal C# control flow.
                </p>
                <a href="/students" class="btn btn-outline-secondary btn-sm">See it on the Student List →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100 border-dark">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white">🧩 Components & Parameters</div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p class="card-text">
                    Break your UI into small, reusable components. Pass data into them using
                    <code>[Parameter]</code> properties and communicate back to the parent with
                    <code>EventCallback</code>.
                </p>
                <a href="/students" class="btn btn-outline-dark btn-sm">See it on the Student List →</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

</div>

